<body><div class=WordSection1><p class=inst>Sardar Vallabhbhai National Institute of Technology (SVNIT), Surat</p><p class=dept>Department of Artificial Intelligence</p><p class=course>Agentic AI (AI465) &mdash; Semester VII</p><hr><table class=g><tr><th>Name</th><th>Roll No.</th><th>Experiment</th><th>Date</th></tr><tr><td>&nbsp;</td><td>U23AI042</td><td>4</td><td>10 September 2026</td></tr></table><h1>Experiment 4</h1><h1 class=sub>Retrieval-Augmented Generation &mdash; Grounding an Agent in a Document Store</h1><h2>Aim</h2><p>To build a retrieval-augmented generation pipeline &mdash; chunk a corpus, vectorise it into a store, retrieve the nearest chunks for a query and assemble a grounded prompt &mdash; and to compare its cited answers against an ungrounded baseline.</p><h2>Theory</h2><p>A language model carries its knowledge in its weights. That knowledge is fixed at training time, cannot be audited, and is produced with the same fluency whether the model has the fact or is inventing it. Retrieval-augmented generation moves the knowledge out of the weights and into a store the developer controls: the corpus is split into chunks, each chunk is turned into a vector, and at query time the chunks nearest the question are fetched and pasted into the prompt as evidence. The model's task narrows from recall to reading comprehension, the corpus can be updated without retraining, and because every retrieved chunk carries its source identifier, each answer can be traced back to the text that supports it.</p><p>This experiment implements the retrieval half completely and scripts the generation step, so the whole pipeline is visible without an API key or network access. The chunks are vectorised with TF-IDF and searched by cosine similarity; substituting dense sentence embeddings and a FAISS index replaces only the vectoriser and the search call, and leaves the chunking, context assembly and citation logic unchanged.</p><p>Note the two limitations visible in the sample run. First, top-k retrieval always returns k chunks, even for a question the corpus cannot answer, so the pipeline will ground an answer in whatever text happened to score highest; a similarity threshold is needed before the pipeline is allowed to answer at all (Exercise 1). Second, retrieval can only return what chunking has made retrievable &mdash; a chunk boundary that cuts an answer in half makes that answer unreachable at any k (Exercise 2). Note also that the pipeline here retrieves exactly once per query; the agentic variant, where the agent itself decides when to retrieve inside a reasoning loop, is taken up in a later experiment.</p><p class=cap>Table 1. Stages of the pipeline and the design choice each one carries.</p><table class=g><tr><th style='width:14%'>Stage</th><th style='width:38%'>What it produces</th><th style='width:48%'>Design choice exercised here</th></tr><tr><td>Chunking</td><td>A list of text chunks, each tagged with its source document and position</td><td>Chunk size and overlap, in sentences</td></tr><tr><td>Vectorisation</td><td>One vector per chunk</td><td>TF-IDF over the corpus vocabulary, with stop words removed</td></tr><tr><td>Indexing</td><td>The vector store &mdash; the chunk matrix searched at query time</td><td>Exhaustive search over a dense matrix, in place of an approximate index</td></tr><tr><td>Retrieval</td><td>The top-k chunks with their similarity scores</td><td>Value of k, and the cosine similarity measure</td></tr><tr><td>Context assembly</td><td>A grounded prompt: instruction, cited chunks, question</td><td>Citation format and the instruction to use only the supplied context</td></tr><tr><td>Generation</td><td>The final answer with its source tag</td><td>Scripted stand-in for the model, so the retrieval is what is being measured</td></tr></table><h2>Algorithm</h2><ol><li>Build the index: split every document in the corpus into overlapping chunks, tag each chunk with its source document and position, vectorise all chunks together, and keep the resulting matrix as the vector store.</li><li>Answer a query: vectorise the query, score it by cosine similarity against every chunk, take the top-k, assemble a grounded prompt from those chunks and their source tags, and print the cited answer next to the answer the same question receives with no retrieval at all.</li></ol><pre class=code style='font-size:8.5pt'>class VectorStore:
    def __init__(self, chunks):
        self.chunks = chunks
        self.vectorizer = TfidfVectorizer(stop_words=&quot;english&quot;)
        self.matrix = self.vectorizer.fit_transform([c[&quot;text&quot;] for c in chunks])

    def search(self, query, k=3):
        q_vec = self.vectorizer.transform([query])
        scores = cosine_similarity(q_vec, self.matrix)[0]
        ranked = sorted(range(len(scores)), key=lambda i: scores[i], reverse=True)
        return [(self.chunks[i], float(scores[i])) for i in ranked[:k]]</pre><br style='page-break-before:always'><h3>Corpus</h3><p>A small fixed corpus of four documents on related but distinct topics (BFS, uniform-cost search, TF-IDF, and RAG itself), checked into <span class=m>docs/</span> as plain <span class=m>.txt</span> files, so that retrieval has real signal to discriminate on and out-of-corpus questions have no chunk that legitimately matches.</p><p class=cap>docs/doc1_bfs.txt</p><pre class=out style='font-size:8.5pt'>Breadth-first search explores a graph level by level, using a FIFO queue to hold the frontier of unvisited nodes. It guarantees the shortest path in terms of number of edges when every edge has the same cost. The algorithm marks each node as visited the first time it is reached, so the first path found to any node is provably the shortest one possible. Breadth-first search expands every node at the current depth before moving to the next depth, which is why it never has to revise a shortest-path decision once it has been made.</pre><p class=cap>docs/doc2_ucs.txt</p><pre class=out style='font-size:8.5pt'>Uniform-cost search generalises breadth-first search to graphs where edges carry different costs. It replaces the FIFO queue with a priority queue ordered by the accumulated path cost from the start node. A node is only finalised when it is popped from the priority queue, at which point its cost is guaranteed to be minimal, because any cheaper path would have been popped earlier. Uniform-cost search reduces to breadth-first search exactly when every edge cost is equal to one.</pre><p class=cap>docs/doc3_tfidf.txt</p><pre class=out style='font-size:8.5pt'>TF-IDF stands for term frequency-inverse document frequency, a weighting scheme used to turn text into numeric vectors. Term frequency counts how often a word appears in a document, while inverse document frequency down-weights words that appear in many documents across the corpus, since common words carry little distinguishing information. Multiplying the two together gives high weight to words that are frequent in one document but rare across the whole corpus, which is exactly the signal that identifies what a document is about. Cosine similarity between two TF-IDF vectors measures the angle between them, ignoring vector length, which makes it a natural way to compare documents of different sizes.</pre><p class=cap>docs/doc4_rag.txt</p><pre class=out style='font-size:8.5pt'>Retrieval-augmented generation combines a retrieval system with a language model so that answers are grounded in an external document store rather than the model&#x27;s fixed training weights. A corpus is first split into overlapping chunks, and each chunk is embedded into a vector using a scheme such as TF-IDF or dense sentence embeddings. At query time, the question is embedded with the same scheme and compared against every chunk using a similarity measure, and the top-k most similar chunks are retrieved. Those chunks are pasted into a prompt as evidence, along with an instruction to answer using only the supplied context, which lets the final answer cite the exact source chunk it came from. Because the corpus lives outside the model, it can be updated at any time without retraining, and answers can be checked against the retrieved text for accuracy.</pre><p>Loaded by <span class=m>corpus.py</span>:</p><pre class=code style='font-size:7.5pt'>&quot;&quot;&quot;
Small fixed corpus for Experiment 4 (Retrieval-Augmented Generation).

Four short documents on related but distinct topics, checked into
docs/ as plain .txt files, so that TF-IDF retrieval has real signal to
work with and questions outside the corpus have no chunk that
legitimately matches.
&quot;&quot;&quot;

import os

DOCS_DIR = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(__file__), &quot;docs&quot;)

DOC_NAMES = [&quot;doc1_bfs.txt&quot;, &quot;doc2_ucs.txt&quot;, &quot;doc3_tfidf.txt&quot;, &quot;doc4_rag.txt&quot;]


def _load(name):
    with open(os.path.join(DOCS_DIR, name), encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;) as f:
        return f.read().strip()


DOCUMENTS = {name: _load(name) for name in DOC_NAMES}</pre><br style='page-break-before:always'><h2>Procedure and Result</h2><h3>Program &mdash; exp4_rag_pipeline.py</h3><pre class=code style='font-size:7.5pt'>&quot;&quot;&quot;
Experiment 4 - Retrieval-Augmented Generation

Implements the retrieval half of a RAG pipeline completely and scripts the
generation step, so the whole pipeline is visible without an API key or
network access:

  1. Chunking    - split each document into overlapping sentence windows,
                   tagging each chunk with its source and position.
  2. Vectorising - TF-IDF over the corpus vocabulary (stop words removed).
  3. Indexing    - keep the TF-IDF matrix as the vector store (exhaustive
                   search, no approximate index).
  4. Retrieval   - cosine-similarity top-k chunks for a query.
  5. Context     - assemble a grounded prompt: instruction + cited chunks
     assembly     + question.
  6. Generation  - a scripted stand-in for the model that answers only from
                   the retrieved chunks, plus an ungrounded baseline that
                   answers from a tiny fixed &quot;parametric memory&quot; instead.
&quot;&quot;&quot;

import os
import re

from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer
from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity

from corpus import DOCUMENTS

OUT_DIR = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(__file__), &quot;outputs&quot;)
os.makedirs(OUT_DIR, exist_ok=True)

DEFAULT_CHUNK_SIZE = 2     # sentences per chunk
DEFAULT_OVERLAP = 1        # sentences of overlap between consecutive chunks


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Chunking
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def split_sentences(text):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Naive sentence splitter: split on &#x27;.&#x27;, drop empties, keep no trailing dot.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    parts = re.split(r&quot;(?&lt;=[.!?])\s+&quot;, text.strip())
    return [p.strip() for p in parts if p.strip()]


def chunk_document(name, text, chunk_size=DEFAULT_CHUNK_SIZE, overlap=DEFAULT_OVERLAP):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Return a list of chunk dicts: {source, position, text}.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    sentences = split_sentences(text)
    step = max(chunk_size - overlap, 1)
    chunks = []
    pos = 0
    i = 0
    while i &lt; len(sentences):
        window = sentences[i:i + chunk_size]
        if not window:
            break
        chunks.append({
            &quot;source&quot;: name,
            &quot;position&quot;: pos,
            &quot;text&quot;: &quot; &quot;.join(window),
        })
        pos += 1
        if i + chunk_size &gt;= len(sentences):
            break
        i += step
    return chunks


def build_chunks(documents=DOCUMENTS, chunk_size=DEFAULT_CHUNK_SIZE, overlap=DEFAULT_OVERLAP):
    chunks = []
    for name, text in documents.items():
        chunks.extend(chunk_document(name, text, chunk_size, overlap))
    return chunks


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2 &amp; 3. Vectorisation + indexing (the &quot;vector store&quot;)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

class VectorStore:
    def __init__(self, chunks):
        self.chunks = chunks
        self.vectorizer = TfidfVectorizer(stop_words=&quot;english&quot;)
        self.matrix = self.vectorizer.fit_transform([c[&quot;text&quot;] for c in chunks])

    def search(self, query, k=3):
        &quot;&quot;&quot;Return the top-k (chunk, score) pairs for a query, best first.&quot;&quot;&quot;
        q_vec = self.vectorizer.transform([query])
        scores = cosine_similarity(q_vec, self.matrix)[0]
        ranked = sorted(range(len(scores)), key=lambda i: scores[i], reverse=True)
        return [(self.chunks[i], float(scores[i])) for i in ranked[:k]]


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Context assembly
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def assemble_context(retrieved):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Build the grounded prompt text from retrieved (chunk, score) pairs.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    lines = [&quot;Answer the question using ONLY the context below. &quot;
             &quot;Cite the source tag you used.&quot;, &quot;&quot;]
    for chunk, score in retrieved:
        tag = f&quot;[{chunk[&#x27;source&#x27;]}#{chunk[&#x27;position&#x27;]}]&quot;
        lines.append(f&quot;{tag} (score={score:.3f}): {chunk[&#x27;text&#x27;]}&quot;)
    return &quot;\n&quot;.join(lines)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Generation - scripted stand-ins
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A real model would read the assembled context and produce these answers;
# here they are scripted so the retrieval step is what&#x27;s being measured.

GROUNDED_ANSWERS = {
    &quot;What data structure does breadth-first search use for its frontier?&quot;:
        &quot;A FIFO queue [doc1_bfs.txt#0].&quot;,
    &quot;How does uniform-cost search decide a node&#x27;s cost is final?&quot;:
        &quot;A node&#x27;s cost is final only when it is popped from the priority &quot;
        &quot;queue, since any cheaper path would already have been popped &quot;
        &quot;[doc2_ucs.txt#0].&quot;,
    &quot;What does the inverse document frequency part of TF-IDF do?&quot;:
        &quot;It down-weights words that appear in many documents, since common &quot;
        &quot;words carry little distinguishing information [doc3_tfidf.txt#0].&quot;,
    &quot;Why can a RAG system be updated without retraining the model?&quot;:
        &quot;Because the corpus lives outside the model&#x27;s weights, in an &quot;
        &quot;external document store that can be edited directly &quot;
        &quot;[doc4_rag.txt#3].&quot;,
}

# Deliberately unanswerable from this corpus (used again in Exercise 1).
UNANSWERABLE_QUESTIONS = [
    &quot;What is the time complexity of quicksort in the worst case?&quot;,
    &quot;Who invented the transformer architecture?&quot;,
]

BASELINE_PARAMETRIC_MEMORY = {
    &quot;What data structure does breadth-first search use for its frontier?&quot;:
        &quot;A queue (this is common textbook knowledge, no source).&quot;,
    &quot;How does uniform-cost search decide a node&#x27;s cost is final?&quot;:
        &quot;When it is dequeued from the priority queue (recalled from &quot;
        &quot;training data, no source).&quot;,
    &quot;What does the inverse document frequency part of TF-IDF do?&quot;:
        &quot;It penalises common words (recalled from training data, no source).&quot;,
    &quot;Why can a RAG system be updated without retraining the model?&quot;:
        &quot;Because you just edit the documents (recalled from training data, &quot;
        &quot;no source).&quot;,
    &quot;What is the time complexity of quicksort in the worst case?&quot;:
        &quot;O(n^2) in the worst case (recalled from training data, no source; &quot;
        &quot;not grounded in this corpus).&quot;,
    &quot;Who invented the transformer architecture?&quot;:
        &quot;Researchers at Google introduced it in &#x27;Attention Is All You Need&#x27; &quot;
        &quot;(recalled from training data, no source; not grounded in this &quot;
        &quot;corpus).&quot;,
}


def ungrounded_answer(question):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;The same question answered with no retrieval at all.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    return BASELINE_PARAMETRIC_MEMORY.get(
        question, &quot;I don&#x27;t know (no parametric memory scripted for this question).&quot;)


def grounded_answer(question, retrieved):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Scripted &#x27;model&#x27; that only ever repeats a pre-written cited answer.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    return GROUNDED_ANSWERS.get(
        question,
        &quot;The retrieved context does not contain an answer to this question.&quot;)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Driver
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

QUERIES = list(GROUNDED_ANSWERS.keys()) + UNANSWERABLE_QUESTIONS


def run_pipeline(k=3, chunk_size=DEFAULT_CHUNK_SIZE, overlap=DEFAULT_OVERLAP, emit=print):
    chunks = build_chunks(chunk_size=chunk_size, overlap=overlap)
    store = VectorStore(chunks)

    emit(f&quot;Indexed {len(chunks)} chunks from {len(DOCUMENTS)} documents &quot;
         f&quot;(chunk_size={chunk_size} sentences, overlap={overlap} sentences).&quot;)
    emit()

    for q in QUERIES:
        emit(f&quot;=== Query: {q} ===&quot;)
        retrieved = store.search(q, k=k)
        for rank, (chunk, score) in enumerate(retrieved, 1):
            emit(f&quot;  rank {rank}: [{chunk[&#x27;source&#x27;]}#{chunk[&#x27;position&#x27;]}] &quot;
                 f&quot;score={score:.3f}  \&quot;{chunk[&#x27;text&#x27;]}\&quot;&quot;)

        context = assemble_context(retrieved)
        emit(&quot;  --- assembled context (truncated) ---&quot;)
        for line in context.splitlines()[:2]:
            emit(f&quot;  {line}&quot;)
        emit(&quot;  ...&quot;)

        emit(f&quot;  GROUNDED answer   : {grounded_answer(q, retrieved)}&quot;)
        emit(f&quot;  UNGROUNDED answer : {ungrounded_answer(q)}&quot;)
        emit()

    return chunks, store


def main():
    lines = []

    def emit(s=&quot;&quot;):
        print(s)
        lines.append(s)

    run_pipeline(k=3, emit=emit)

    out_path = os.path.join(OUT_DIR, &quot;exp4_rag_pipeline.txt&quot;)
    with open(out_path, &quot;w&quot;) as f:
        f.write(&quot;\n&quot;.join(lines) + &quot;\n&quot;)
    print(f&quot;[saved output to {out_path}]&quot;)


if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    main()</pre><br style='page-break-before:always'><h3>Output</h3><p class=cap>Snapshot of the console output (Python 3.12.1 on Windows (AMD64), executed 10 September 2026, 11:19), including the number of chunks indexed and, for every query, the retrieved chunks with their similarity scores, the grounded answer with citation, and the ungrounded baseline.</p><pre class=out style='font-size:6.5pt'>Indexed 13 chunks from 4 documents (chunk_size=2 sentences, overlap=1 sentences).

=== Query: What data structure does breadth-first search use for its frontier? ===
