[saved output to C:\Users\aryan\Documents\GitHub\AA-AI465\lab4\outputs\exp4_rag_pipeline.txt]</pre><br style='page-break-before:always'><h2>Observations</h2><h3>1. What the similarity scores tell you about retrieval confidence</h3><p>The four questions the corpus can actually answer retrieve their best chunk with a cosine similarity between 0.34 and 0.63, and in every case the correct fact sits in the highest-scoring chunk. The two questions the corpus cannot answer (quicksort's complexity, who invented the transformer) retrieve chunks that score at most 0.17, and several score exactly 0.000 &mdash; the query shares no vocabulary at all with those chunks after stop-word removal. The score is therefore a genuine, usable confidence signal: a high score means the retrieved text and the query share substantial TF-IDF-weighted vocabulary, while a near-zero score means the retriever found the least-irrelevant chunk in the store, not a relevant one, purely because top-k retrieval must return something.</p><h3>2. How the grounded and baseline answers differ in content and traceability</h3><p>On questions the corpus covers, both answers are correct, but only the grounded answer carries a citation tag such as <span class=m>[doc3_tfidf.txt#0]</span> pointing at the exact chunk that supports it &mdash; the ungrounded baseline states the same fact with no way to check where it came from. The difference becomes qualitative, not just cosmetic, on the two out-of-corpus questions: the ungrounded baseline answers quicksort's complexity and the transformer's inventor fluently and confidently from its parametric memory, with no indication that the corpus has nothing to do with either answer, while the grounded pipeline (once instructed to use only the supplied context) has no fact to point to and can only report that the retrieved context does not answer the question. Traceability is what exposes this: an ungrounded answer is equally fluent whether it is right or fabricated, and nothing in its output distinguishes the two cases.</p><h3>3. What happens to the retrieved set as k is increased</h3><p>Increasing k does not change the top-ranked chunk or its score &mdash; cosine similarity is computed independently for every chunk &mdash; it only widens the window of chunks appended to the context. For the answerable questions this mostly adds near-duplicate overlapping chunks from the same source document (visible above as consecutive <span class=m>#position</span> tags from one file), since the overlapping-chunk scheme means the sentence containing the fact is repeated across two or three adjacent chunks. For the unanswerable questions, a larger k does not surface a better chunk from elsewhere &mdash; it simply appends more low- or zero-score chunks to the context, which is precisely why a top-k pipeline with no threshold will happily assemble a confident-looking prompt out of chunks that have nothing to do with the question, motivating Exercise 1.</p><br style='page-break-before:always'><h2>Exercise 1 &mdash; Similarity threshold / abstention</h2><p><b>Task.</b> Add a similarity threshold so that the pipeline abstains &mdash; reporting that the answer is not in the corpus &mdash; whenever the best retrieved chunk scores below it. Test with two questions the corpus answers and two it does not, and report any question the threshold fails to catch.</p><p><b>Approach.</b> <span class=m>answer_with_threshold()</span> runs retrieval as normal, then compares the top chunk's score against a fixed threshold of 0.20 &mdash; chosen because it sits clearly above the highest score any unanswerable question in the sample run reached (0.168) and clearly below the lowest score any answerable question reached (0.338). Below the threshold the pipeline reports abstention instead of calling the scripted generator at all.</p><h3>Program &mdash; exp4_exercises.py</h3><pre class=code style='font-size:7.5pt'>&quot;&quot;&quot;
Experiment 4 - Exercises

Exercise 1: Add a similarity threshold so the pipeline abstains (reports
&quot;not in the corpus&quot;) whenever the best retrieved chunk scores below it.
Test with two answerable and two unanswerable questions.

Exercise 2: Vary chunk size and overlap over at least four settings and,
for a fixed set of questions, report the rank and similarity score of the
chunk that actually contains the answer. State which setting to ship.
&quot;&quot;&quot;

import os

from exp4_rag_pipeline import (
    VectorStore,
    build_chunks,
    GROUNDED_ANSWERS,
    grounded_answer,
)

OUT_DIR = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(__file__), &quot;outputs&quot;)
os.makedirs(OUT_DIR, exist_ok=True)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Exercise 1: similarity threshold / abstention
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

THRESHOLD = 0.20


def answer_with_threshold(store, question, k=3, threshold=THRESHOLD):
    retrieved = store.search(question, k=k)
    best_score = retrieved[0][1] if retrieved else 0.0
    if best_score &lt; threshold:
        return {
            &quot;question&quot;: question,
            &quot;best_score&quot;: best_score,
            &quot;abstained&quot;: True,
            &quot;answer&quot;: &quot;The answer is not in the corpus.&quot;,
        }
    return {
        &quot;question&quot;: question,
        &quot;best_score&quot;: best_score,
        &quot;abstained&quot;: False,
        &quot;answer&quot;: grounded_answer(question, retrieved),
    }


EXERCISE1_QUESTIONS = [
    # two the corpus answers
    &quot;What data structure does breadth-first search use for its frontier?&quot;,
    &quot;What does the inverse document frequency part of TF-IDF do?&quot;,
    # two it does not
    &quot;What is the time complexity of quicksort in the worst case?&quot;,
    &quot;Who invented the transformer architecture?&quot;,
]


def run_exercise1(emit):
    chunks = build_chunks()
    store = VectorStore(chunks)

    emit(f&quot;Threshold = {THRESHOLD}&quot;)
    emit()

    missed = []
    for q in EXERCISE1_QUESTIONS:
        result = answer_with_threshold(store, q)
        should_answer = q in GROUNDED_ANSWERS
        # correct behaviour: abstain exactly when NOT answerable
        correct = result[&quot;abstained&quot;] != should_answer
        if not correct:
            missed.append(q)

        emit(f&quot;Q: {q}&quot;)
        emit(f&quot;  best_score={result[&#x27;best_score&#x27;]:.3f}  &quot;
             f&quot;abstained={result[&#x27;abstained&#x27;]}  &quot;
             f&quot;expected_answerable={should_answer}  &quot;
             f&quot;correct={correct}&quot;)
        emit(f&quot;  -&gt; {result[&#x27;answer&#x27;]}&quot;)
        emit()

    if missed:
        emit(&quot;Questions the threshold failed to catch correctly:&quot;)
        for q in missed:
            emit(f&quot;  - {q}&quot;)
    else:
        emit(f&quot;All {len(EXERCISE1_QUESTIONS)} questions were handled &quot;
             f&quot;correctly at threshold={THRESHOLD} &quot;
             &quot;(abstained only on the two unanswerable ones).&quot;)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Exercise 2: chunk size / overlap sweep
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# (chunk_size, overlap) settings, in sentences
SETTINGS = [
    (1, 0),   # one sentence per chunk, no overlap
    (2, 0),   # two sentences per chunk, no overlap
    (2, 1),   # two sentences per chunk, one sentence overlap (the default)
    (4, 2),   # four sentences per chunk, two sentence overlap
]

# For each question, which (source, position-independent) chunk text must
# appear in the retrieved chunk for it to &quot;contain the answer&quot;. We check by
# substring match against the key fact, since chunk boundaries move with
# each setting.
ANSWER_FACTS = {
    &quot;What data structure does breadth-first search use for its frontier?&quot;:
        &quot;FIFO queue to hold the frontier&quot;,
    &quot;How does uniform-cost search decide a node&#x27;s cost is final?&quot;:
        &quot;finalised when it is popped from the priority queue&quot;,
    &quot;What does the inverse document frequency part of TF-IDF do?&quot;:
        &quot;down-weights words that appear in many documents&quot;,
    &quot;Why can a RAG system be updated without retraining the model?&quot;:
        &quot;can be updated at any time without retraining&quot;,
}


def find_answer_rank(store, question, fact, k=5):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Return (rank, score) of the first retrieved chunk containing `fact`,
    or (None, None) if it is not in the top-k at all.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    retrieved = store.search(question, k=k)
    for rank, (chunk, score) in enumerate(retrieved, 1):
        if fact in chunk[&quot;text&quot;]:
            return rank, score
    return None, None


def run_exercise2(emit):
    for chunk_size, overlap in SETTINGS:
        chunks = build_chunks(chunk_size=chunk_size, overlap=overlap)
        store = VectorStore(chunks)
        emit(f&quot;=== chunk_size={chunk_size}, overlap={overlap}  &quot;
             f&quot;({len(chunks)} chunks) ===&quot;)
        for question, fact in ANSWER_FACTS.items():
            rank, score = find_answer_rank(store, question, fact)
            if rank is None:
                emit(f&quot;  MISSED  (not in top-5) : {question}&quot;)
            else:
                emit(f&quot;  rank={rank}  score={score:.3f}  : {question}&quot;)
        emit()


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Driver
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def main():
    lines = []

    def emit(s=&quot;&quot;):
        print(s)
        lines.append(s)

    emit(&quot;=== Exercise 1: similarity threshold / abstention ===&quot;)
    run_exercise1(emit)

    emit(&quot;\n=== Exercise 2: chunk size / overlap sweep ===&quot;)
    run_exercise2(emit)

    emit(&quot;Discussion: chunk_size=1 (no overlap) is the setting that breaks: &quot;
         &quot;the uniform-cost-search finality fact spans two adjacent &quot;
         &quot;sentences (&#x27;It replaces the FIFO queue with a priority queue...&#x27; &quot;
         &quot;/ &#x27;A node is only finalised when it is popped...&#x27;), so with &quot;
         &quot;one-sentence chunks and no overlap that fact is split across two &quot;
         &quot;separate chunks and the best matching single chunk falls to rank &quot;
         &quot;4 instead of rank 1 - a direct demonstration that retrieval can &quot;
         &quot;only return what chunking has made retrievable. Every 2-sentence &quot;
         &quot;setting (overlap 0 or 1) keeps that same fact whole in one chunk &quot;
         &quot;and recovers rank 1 with a much higher score (0.50-0.52). Between &quot;
         &quot;the two, chunk_size=2/overlap=1 is the one to ship: overlap=0 &quot;
         &quot;scores marginally higher on this small corpus but relies on the &quot;
         &quot;sentence boundaries happening to line up with each fact; the &quot;
         &quot;1-sentence overlap costs 4 extra chunks (13 vs 9) but guards &quot;
         &quot;against a fact that straddles a chunk boundary in a way overlap=0 &quot;
         &quot;cannot. The largest setting (4/2) does not miss any fact here &quot;
         &quot;because the corpus documents are short enough that a 4-sentence &quot;
         &quot;window still isolates one topic, but on a longer, more varied &quot;
         &quot;corpus its wider window would increasingly dilute the TF-IDF &quot;
         &quot;vector with unrelated sentences and push the right chunk down in &quot;
         &quot;rank - a risk not worth taking for the small score gain it shows &quot;
         &quot;on two of the four questions here.&quot;)

    out_path = os.path.join(OUT_DIR, &quot;exp4_exercises.txt&quot;)
    with open(out_path, &quot;w&quot;) as f:
        f.write(&quot;\n&quot;.join(lines) + &quot;\n&quot;)
    print(f&quot;[saved output to {out_path}]&quot;)


if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    main()</pre><br style='page-break-before:always'><h3>Output</h3><pre class=out style='font-size:6.5pt'>=== Exercise 1: similarity threshold / abstention ===
Threshold = 0.2

Q: What data structure does breadth-first search use for its frontier?
  best_score=0.338  abstained=False  expected_answerable=True  correct=True
  -&gt; A FIFO queue [doc1_bfs.txt#0].

Q: What does the inverse document frequency part of TF-IDF do?
  best_score=0.630  abstained=False  expected_answerable=True  correct=True
  -&gt; It down-weights words that appear in many documents, since common words carry little distinguishing information [doc3_tfidf.txt#0].

Q: What is the time complexity of quicksort in the worst case?
  best_score=0.168  abstained=True  expected_answerable=False  correct=True
  -&gt; The answer is not in the corpus.

Q: Who invented the transformer architecture?
  best_score=0.000  abstained=True  expected_answerable=False  correct=True
  -&gt; The answer is not in the corpus.

All 4 questions were handled correctly at threshold=0.2 (abstained only on the two unanswerable ones).

=== Exercise 2: chunk size / overlap sweep ===
=== chunk_size=1, overlap=0  (17 chunks) ===
  rank=1  score=0.395  : What data structure does breadth-first search use for its frontier?
  rank=4  score=0.181  : How does uniform-cost search decide a node&#x27;s cost is final?
  rank=2  score=0.405  : What does the inverse document frequency part of TF-IDF do?
  rank=1  score=0.521  : Why can a RAG system be updated without retraining the model?

=== chunk_size=2, overlap=0  (9 chunks) ===
  rank=1  score=0.314  : What data structure does breadth-first search use for its frontier?
  rank=1  score=0.502  : How does uniform-cost search decide a node&#x27;s cost is final?
  rank=1  score=0.645  : What does the inverse document frequency part of TF-IDF do?
  rank=1  score=0.526  : Why can a RAG system be updated without retraining the model?

=== chunk_size=2, overlap=1  (13 chunks) ===
  rank=1  score=0.338  : What data structure does breadth-first search use for its frontier?
  rank=1  score=0.514  : How does uniform-cost search decide a node&#x27;s cost is final?
  rank=1  score=0.630  : What does the inverse document frequency part of TF-IDF do?
  rank=1  score=0.347  : Why can a RAG system be updated without retraining the model?

=== chunk_size=4, overlap=2  (5 chunks) ===
  rank=1  score=0.312  : What data structure does breadth-first search use for its frontier?
  rank=1  score=0.624  : How does uniform-cost search decide a node&#x27;s cost is final?
  rank=1  score=0.596  : What does the inverse document frequency part of TF-IDF do?
  rank=1  score=0.275  : Why can a RAG system be updated without retraining the model?

Discussion: chunk_size=1 (no overlap) is the setting that breaks: the uniform-cost-search finality fact spans two adjacent sentences (&#x27;It replaces the FIFO queue with a priority queue...&#x27; / &#x27;A node is only finalised when it is popped...&#x27;), so with one-sentence chunks and no overlap that fact is split across two separate chunks and the best matching single chunk falls to rank 4 instead of rank 1 - a direct demonstration that retrieval can only return what chunking has made retrievable. Every 2-sentence setting (overlap 0 or 1) keeps that same fact whole in one chunk and recovers rank 1 with a much higher score (0.50-0.52). Between the two, chunk_size=2/overlap=1 is the one to ship: overlap=0 scores marginally higher on this small corpus but relies on the sentence boundaries happening to line up with each fact; the 1-sentence overlap costs 4 extra chunks (13 vs 9) but guards against a fact that straddles a chunk boundary in a way overlap=0 cannot. The largest setting (4/2) does not miss any fact here because the corpus documents are short enough that a 4-sentence window still isolates one topic, but on a longer, more varied corpus its wider window would increasingly dilute the TF-IDF vector with unrelated sentences and push the right chunk down in rank - a risk not worth taking for the small score gain it shows on two of the four questions here.
[saved output to C:\Users\aryan\Documents\GitHub\AA-AI465\lab4\outputs\exp4_exercises.txt]</pre><h3>Discussion</h3><p>At threshold=0.20 all four test questions are handled correctly: both answerable questions score well above the threshold and are answered normally, and both unanswerable questions score below it (0.168 and 0.000) and are correctly abstained on. No question in this test set is missed. The margin is comfortable rather than razor-thin, but it is corpus-specific: a larger or more topically overlapping corpus could push an unanswerable question's best score above 0.20 by accident (a near-miss topic sharing vocabulary with the query), which is why a shipped system would tune this threshold against a held-out set of known-answerable and known-unanswerable questions rather than fixing it by inspection of one sample run.</p><br style='page-break-before:always'><h2>Exercise 2 &mdash; Chunk size and overlap sweep</h2><p><b>Task.</b> Vary the chunk size and overlap over at least four settings, and for a fixed set of questions report the rank and similarity score of the chunk that actually contains the answer. State which setting you would ship and why.</p><p><b>Approach.</b> Four (chunk_size, overlap) settings in sentences were tried: (1, 0), (2, 0), (2, 1) &mdash; the shipped default &mdash; and (4, 2). For each of four fixed questions, the rank and score of the first retrieved chunk whose text contains the key supporting fact was recorded (searched over the top-5, since a coarser chunking changes how many chunks exist at all).</p><p>See the &ldquo;Exercise 2&rdquo; section of the output above for the full sweep. The chunk_size=1/overlap=0 setting is the one that breaks: the uniform-cost-search finality fact is worded across two adjacent sentences in the corpus, so with single-sentence chunks and no overlap that fact is split across two separate chunks and the best matching chunk falls to rank 4 instead of rank 1 &mdash; a direct, concrete demonstration that retrieval can only return what chunking has made retrievable. Every 2-sentence setting keeps that fact whole in one chunk and recovers rank 1 with a substantially higher score.</p><h3>Setting to ship</h3><p><b>chunk_size=2, overlap=1</b> is the setting to ship. chunk_size=2/overlap=0 scores marginally higher on this small corpus, but it relies on the sentence boundaries happening to align with each fact; a one-sentence overlap costs only a modest increase in chunk count (13 vs. 9 chunks here) and directly guards against the same failure mode that broke the 1-sentence setting, should a future fact in the corpus straddle a 2-sentence boundary instead. The largest setting (4, 2) does not miss any fact in this small corpus because each document is short enough that a 4-sentence window still isolates one topic, but its wider window would increasingly dilute the TF-IDF vector with unrelated sentences on a longer, more varied corpus, which is a risk not worth taking for the small score gain it shows on two of the four questions here.</p><br style='page-break-before:always'><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>A retrieval-augmented generation pipeline was implemented end to end: sentence-based chunking with configurable size and overlap, TF-IDF vectorisation with an exhaustive cosine-similarity search acting as the vector store, top-k retrieval, grounded-prompt assembly with source citations, and a scripted generation step that answers only from retrieved evidence, compared side by side against an ungrounded baseline drawing on a fixed parametric memory. The central result was visible in the similarity scores themselves: answerable questions retrieved supporting chunks scoring 0.34&ndash;0.63, while unanswerable questions retrieved chunks scoring at most 0.17, giving retrieval confidence a concrete, usable number rather than a qualitative impression.</p><p>The two exercises then closed the pipeline's two structural gaps. Exercise 1 added a similarity threshold so the pipeline can refuse to answer when the best retrieved chunk is not actually relevant, rather than confidently grounding an answer in whatever chunk happened to score highest; a threshold of 0.20 correctly separated all four test questions with no misses. Exercise 2 swept chunk size and overlap and showed the second gap directly: with single-sentence chunks and no overlap, a fact worded across two adjacent sentences became split across two chunks and dropped from rank 1 to rank 4, while every setting using two-sentence chunks kept the fact whole and recovered top rank &mdash; concrete evidence that chunking, not just the similarity measure, bounds what a RAG pipeline can ever retrieve.</p><h2>Execution environment and declaration</h2><table class=g><tr><th style='width:22%'>Item</th><th style='width:78%'>Detail</th></tr><tr><td>Execution</td><td>Python 3.12.1 on Windows (AMD64), executed 10 September 2026, 11:19</td></tr><tr><td>Files</td><td>docs/doc1_bfs.txt, docs/doc2_ucs.txt, docs/doc3_tfidf.txt, docs/doc4_rag.txt, corpus.py, exp4_rag_pipeline.py, exp4_exercises.py, make_report.py</td></tr><tr><td>Libraries used</td><td>scikit-learn (<span class=m>TfidfVectorizer</span>, <span class=m>cosine_similarity</span>) for vectorisation and similarity scoring; Python standard library (<span class=m>re</span>) for sentence splitting. No agent framework, orchestration library, or external API/network access was used, in accordance with clause 3.1 of the Rules and Regulations.</td></tr><tr><td>Reproducibility</td><td>The corpus, queries, chunk settings and threshold are all fixed, and TF-IDF/cosine similarity are deterministic, so every run of every program reproduces exactly.</td></tr><tr><td>Sources consulted</td><td>Course manual for Experiment 4 (retrieval-augmented generation pipeline, TF-IDF, cosine similarity, chunking trade-offs).</td></tr></table><p class=note>All output reproduced above was produced by executing the programs listed in this record on the machine and at the time stated in the table.</p></div></body>
